## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate a concrete log-routing bug into a CAPA program that scales across tenants and flags, including governance, measurability, and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, lineage, owners
- Tenant-aware lifecycle with detect/contain/remediate/verify and SLAs
- REST API design for CAPA management with logs/traces and policy links
- Metrics: containment MTTC, recurrence rate per tenant
- RCA template and canary rollout plan for config changes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example CAPA data model (TypeScript)
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  issue: string;
  tenantId: string;
  evidence: string[]; // logId, traceId
  lineage: string[]; // [source, sink]
  owners: string[];
  severity: 'low'|'medium'|'high';
  status: 'open'|'in_progress'|'mitigated'|'verified';
  timestamps: { detected: string; closed?: string };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test containment and prevention at scale?
- How would you secure CAPA data access and auditability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CAPA design for a real multi-tenant data routing bug with data residency risk, ensuring traceability from detection to verification and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, tenant IDs, and route metadata
- Region-aware lifecycle state machine with clear escalations
- Linked logs/traces in CAPA records for auditability
- Canary rollout and RCA templates with measurable metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal REST API skeleton for CAPA creation
app.post('/capa', (req, res) => {
  const { evidence, tenantId, routeInfo } = req.body;
  // persist CAPA and link logs/traces
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'capa-123', status: 'open' });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify false positives and tune thresholds across regions?
- What automated checks would prevent reintroduction of the misrouting in future deployments?

